+
+## ADR-0208 — The XAML audits ADR-0191 promised are tests, and a binding beside a `DataContext` states its source
+
+**Status.** Accepted, 2026-09-04.
+
+**Context.** ADR-0191 ended with "where a class of defect can be caught statically, it now is: a
+`StaticResource` audit over the views, and a scan for `DataContext` overrides that shadow a sibling
+binding, both run clean over every view in the repository." That was true on the day and left nothing
+behind. There was no script, no test and no gate — the pass was somebody reading files once.
+
+The consequence arrived immediately and is recorded in this repository's own handoff: the tab-strip
+idiom ADR-0191 named as fixed in Employees was still wrong in Customers and Finance a slice later,
+carried forward as a known defect. It was also wrong in **Purchasing** and **Warehouse**, which
+nobody had noticed at all, because nothing was looking. Across the four, twenty-seven buttons in
+four workspaces rendered with nothing selected, in every theme, silently.
+
+A second instance of the shadowing class was found the same way and had never been recorded:
+`StocktakesView` and `TransfersView` each bind a detail card's `Visibility` to `IsDetailOpen` on the
+element that also sets `DataContext="{Binding Detail}"`. `IsDetailOpen` belongs to
+`WarehouseCountsViewModel` and `WarehouseTransfersViewModel`; the detail view models do not have it.
+The binding resolved against the child, failed, and left `Visibility` at its default — a detail card
+permanently open over a register with nothing selected. Exactly the Finance defect, in a workspace
+whose Functional Alpha had already been claimed.
+
+An audit nobody can run is a decision that expires.
+
+**Decision.** `XamlContractTests` in `Qrafig.Desktop.Tests` is that audit, executable. It reads every
+`.xaml` under `desktop/src/Qrafig.Desktop`, blanks XML comments so a file that *explains* a defect is
+not read as *having* one, and asserts five things: every `StaticResource` key is defined somewhere;
+no control carries a keyed style whose `TargetType` names a different control; no property `Trigger`
+compares `Tag` against a boolean; every binding beside a `DataContext` override states its own source;
+and every list control has a style or an implicit one. A sixth test asserts that the audit actually
+found the views, because the other five pass trivially over an empty list and a renamed folder would
+otherwise turn the file green while auditing nothing.
+
+It is **file scanning, not WPF**. No `PresentationCore` reference, no dispatcher, no window — so it
+runs in the headless suite, on any platform, in about a tenth of a second.
+
+**The `DataContext` rule is structural, and that is the decision.** The narrow alternative — "bind to
+a property the child actually has" — is correct in any single case and unenforceable across all of
+them, because it requires the audit to know every view model. Requiring an explicit `RelativeSource`,
+`ElementName` or `Source` on any binding sharing an element with a `DataContext` override makes the
+intent readable at the point of use and the audit exact. Fifteen of the repository's seventeen
+`DataContext` overrides already complied; the two that did not were the two defects.
+
+**Consequences.** The Desktop suite gains six tests and the repository gains a gate that cannot be
+forgotten, because it fails a build rather than depending on somebody remembering ADR-0191. All four
+tab strips now use the `DataTrigger` form Employees introduced, and the two detail cards are rooted
+through their `UserControl`.
+
+This does **not** replace the live `QRAFIG.exe` smoke and must not be read as doing so. The audit sees
+names and shapes. It cannot see layout, clipping, focus, virtualization, a command that never re-asks
+whether it may execute, or a control bound to nothing — which is most of what the live run is for.
+ADR-0191's gate stands unchanged; this is the part of it that no longer needs a person.
